WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.859
Leah: As we have already discussed previously
choosing the right deployment strategy for a

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.340
project would include several considerations.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:11.110
One serious consideration is
it’s support with rollback.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.810
Chris: Rollback is the process of ‘rolling
back’ a software product to a previous version.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.550
This normally occurs when there is an issue
with the current release [the latest version]

00:00:21.009 --> 00:00:23.730
such as several bug fixes that need to be done.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.470
It may be a simple process if it only involves
small changes: However, it could become an issue

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.750
if it involves several elements of a software
project, as they may have multiple dependencies.

00:00:35.549 --> 00:00:39.880
It would also become an issue, if the deployment
strategy choice, such as Basic deployment,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.780
makes it difficult to support rollback.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:47.059
Leah: Rollbacks can be fully automated,
this is where a system would examine

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.449
a set measurement or metric, and would
either, mark it as complete which would

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.010
finish the deployment, or roll it back.

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:59.190
Rollbacks need to be quick, so as not to
interrupt the end user using the product,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.369
and if the product uses a database, it
will need to ensure that no data is lost.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:08.610
Chris: For deployments to be successful,
a rollback strategy would need to be

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.660
considered in the Deployment plan.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.490
This written document outlines all the
steps required to recover an application

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:21.070
and or database to the point before the
deployment, outlining the specific steps

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.350
for each member of the deployment team.

00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:28.260
The plan would be tested so all the
components could be determined, and it

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.410
would include the locations of the scripts,
needed to ‘rollback’ the deployment.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.780
It would also include the database name and the
developer responsible for running the scripts.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.359
Leah: When something does go wrong with
a deployment, a team is more likely

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.800
to respond in a systematic, calm, and
logical way if it has a rollback strategy.

